# Default: let Git detect text files and normalize committed text to LF.
* text=auto

# Cross-platform source: keep Python blobs and checkouts LF on every OS.
*.py text eol=lf
*.pyi text eol=lf

# Repo control files should not drift by writer platform.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.ini text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.cfg text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Committed data/ruling stores are byte-stable records, not text to normalize.
data/** -text !eol

# Same contract, same reason: recorded fixtures (correction/parity/replay stores)
# and generated tuning-run reports are RECORDS, written by whichever OS produced
# them. `* text=auto` above was already leaving them alone — but only by the
# grandfathering rule that spares blobs already committed with CRLF, which is an
# accident of history rather than a declared intent, and which a repo-wide
# `git add --renormalize .` would override in one stroke. Stated explicitly so the
# protection survives that command.
tests/fixtures/** -text !eol
docs/tuning/runs/** -text !eol

# Native engine wrapper is vendored/off-limits; preserve bytes exactly.
src/cg/** -text !eol

# A deterministic BUILD artifact, not a record: `build_card_functions.shipped_bytes` is the one
# writer, so the byte-identity test (`test_the_shipped_store_is_byte_identical_to_what_the_builder_
# writes`) is written to hold on any OS the builder runs on — unlike the `data/**` family above,
# whose committed bytes are inherently whichever OS produced that run. Under bare `text=auto` this
# store still checked out CRLF on Windows (`core.eol` defaults to `native`; only an explicit `eol=`
# attribute or `core.autocrlf=true/input` forces normalization on checkout, and neither was set),
# so the test the builder's own docstring names as the guard (Issue #395 D6.4, cf. ADR-0116) was
# red on every Windows checkout despite the blob itself being clean LF. `eol=lf` closes it the same
# way `*.py` is closed above. Scoped to this one file rather than `src/common/*.json`: the sibling
# `attack_overrides.json` deliberately tolerates "whichever OS runs this" per its own test docstring
# (`tests/sim/test_generate_attack_overrides.py`), so a directory-wide rule would fight that file's
# stated design rather than fix a defect.
src/common/card_functions.json text eol=lf

# The same contract for the same reason, one store over. `build_card_effects.py` is the one writer
# for `card_effects.json` and also writes LF unconditionally, so on a Windows checkout (`core.eol`
# = `native`) every re-stamp rewrote all ~1300 lines and buried a two-entry edit in a whole-file
# diff. The blob is already clean LF, so this rule changes ZERO committed bytes — it only stops the
# checkout from disagreeing with the builder. Added by Issue #394, whose item F1 re-stamps this
# store; PR #411 fixed the sibling and this file was simply not in its scope. Note the CI guard
# cannot catch either case: `ci.yml` matches `attr/text eol=lf` on the checked-out attribute, which
# is exactly what is missing when the rule is absent.
src/common/card_effects.json text eol=lf

# Common binary artifacts.
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.pyd binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.7z binary
*.tar binary
*.tgz binary
*.parquet binary
*.pickle binary
*.pkl binary
